Order matters:
	Because cryptographic hashing algorithms like SHA-256 process data as a sequential stream of bytes, 
        changing the order of even a single character alters the mathematical path entirely.

avalanche effect:
	This is a fundamental security property called the avalanche effect—even the tiniest change in the input (like swapping 
	two letters or changing a lowercase to an uppercase letter) results in a completely unrecognizable, radically different hash.


Why this is perfect for file ingestion pipeline:
	This is exactly what you want when preventing duplicate documents in your database. If a user uploads a text file, 
	and later someone rearranges the sentences inside that file without adding any new words, the content is technically 
	different.Because order matters to SHA-256, your script will correctly identify it as a modified version (a new hash) 
	rather than treating it as the exact same file.
